{"question":"What is causing the persistent upward shift in pendulum_obs beat counts relative to the day 1–10 baseline, together with mildly increased scatter and occasional high outliers, while resonator_obs remains essentially unchanged relative to its own baseline?","hypotheses":[{"label":"Small environmental or setup drift affecting the pendulum apparatus only","probability":0.58,"rationale":"This remains the best fit to the evidence I have actually measured. The pendulum mean is still elevated over a long post-baseline stretch, and the newer Day 25–28 readings continue to sit mostly above the baseline with a few higher excursions. A localized change in temperature, humidity, alignment, mounting, or tension can plausibly shift pendulum timing without moving the resonator, and it can also widen the scatter if conditions vary from session to session. The resonator’s stability argues against a broad observatory-wide effect and supports a more local influence on the pendulum setup.","evidenceFor":[401,423,424,425,426,427,428,429,430,432,433,434,435,436,437,438,439,465,466,467,468,469,470,471,472],"evidenceAgainst":[ ]}
,{"label":"A real mechanical change in the pendulum system itself","probability":0.22,"rationale":"A persistent offset plus somewhat increased dispersion is also compatible with wear, loosening, drift in suspension geometry, or another developing fault in the pendulum apparatus. The fact that the effect is confined to the pendulum and not mirrored in the resonator makes an apparatus-specific change plausible. I keep this below environmental/setup drift because the record still does not show a distinct failure signature, only a sustained shift with mild scatter increase.","evidenceFor":[401,423,424,425,426,427,428,429,430,432,433,434,435,436,437,438,439,465,466,467,468,469,470,471,472],"evidenceAgainst":[ ]}
,{"label":"Ordinary measurement scatter around a mostly stable pendulum baseline","probability":0.08,"rationale":"Some of the post-baseline values remain near the baseline, and a few high values by themselves do not prove a new regime. But the full post-baseline mean remains elevated, and the shift has persisted across many trials, so scatter alone is not a good account of the pattern. The resonator’s lack of a comparable shift also makes a general noise explanation less convincing.","evidenceFor":[432,433,435,437,438,439,465,467,468,470,471,472],"evidenceAgainst":[401,423,424,425,426,427,428,429,430,465,466,469,470]}
,{"label":"A brief transient disturbance affecting some observing sessions or trials","probability":0.04,"rationale":"Transient interference could explain the occasional high outlier and part of the wider spread, but the elevated mean has persisted across multiple days rather than collapsing after one affected session. That makes a short-lived disturbance insufficient as the main explanation, though it may have contributed to individual readings.","evidenceFor":[425,427,428,469],"evidenceAgainst":[401,423,424,426,429,430,432,433,434,435,436,437,438,439,465,466,467,468,470,471,472]}
,{"label":"An unrecognized interaction or other unmodeled factor","probability":0.05,"rationale":"There may be a coupling I have not yet isolated, because the pendulum shows a modest persistent offset with irregular scatter while the resonator does not share the shift. However, I do not yet have a specific mechanism, and the evidence currently favors more mundane local explanations first.","evidenceFor":[401,423,424,425,426,427,428,429,430,432,433,434,435,436,437,438,439,465,466,467,468,469,470,471,472],"evidenceAgainst":[ ]}
,{"label":"Residual: something not yet considered","probability":0.03,"rationale":"The record is still not complete enough to exclude a cause I have not yet thought of. I keep a small residual because the anomaly is real but not uniquely explained.","evidenceFor":[401,423,424,425,426,427,428,429,430,432,433,434,435,436,437,438,439,465,466,467,468,469,470,471,472],"evidenceAgainst":[ ]}
],"residual":0.03,"summaryOfChange":"I kept the same core anomaly because the new Day 25–28 pendulum readings continue the earlier elevated post-baseline pattern, while the resonator remains stable. I increased confidence slightly in a localized pendulum-only cause because the persistence is reinforced by the newest data, and I reduced the weight on pure scatter/transient explanations because the shift is now more clearly sustained across additional days. The residual remains small but nonzero."}
